0317742dbf1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409558 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\cdot \\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\cdot \\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2\\sqrt{xy}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(1,-1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(1)(-1)}}=-{e}^{\\sqrt{-1}}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have a negative number under the radical.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(-1,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(-1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}={e}^{\\sqrt{1}}={e}^{1}=e,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,-1) & =\\frac{(-1){e}^{\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}}{2\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1}}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=-\\frac{e}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1)=1\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{0\\cdot 1}}}{2\\sqrt{0\\cdot 1}}=\\frac{{e}^{0}}{2\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II  only'." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409560 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186409563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409560 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "06a4b700-aa67-595f-ad77-c3a9c2d08fa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409560 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186409558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409561 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186409560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409561 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1786d23c-6b2b-5b25-8a34-a3df54af58bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409561 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186339043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409562 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186409561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409562 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "488b6ab6-cae5-5